WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.473 --> 00:00:04.699
- Hello Believe Nation, it's Evan.

00:00:04.699 --> 00:00:07.123
My own word is believe
and I believe in people

00:00:07.123 --> 00:00:09.053
more than they believe in themselves

00:00:09.053 --> 00:00:11.975
and my sincere hope is that
if you believe in yourself

00:00:11.975 --> 00:00:14.045
as much as I believe in you,

00:00:14.045 --> 00:00:16.720
you'll be able to change the planet.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.719
So to help you on your journey,
today I'm doing a video

00:00:18.719 --> 00:00:21.320
on the 10 skills that are hard to learn

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.403
but will pay off forever.

00:00:24.247 --> 00:00:25.613
And as always guys, if you're watching,

00:00:25.613 --> 00:00:27.725
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:27.725 --> 00:00:29.504
leave it down in the comments below,

00:00:29.504 --> 00:00:31.111
it's much more likely
to stick with yourself

00:00:31.111 --> 00:00:32.677
and if you leave a comment
within the first few hours

00:00:32.677 --> 00:00:34.710
of the video going live,
you have a chance to win

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:36.774
one of two daily prizes. Enjoy!

00:00:36.774 --> 00:00:39.441
Skill number one is speaking up.

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:44.891
- One of the things you
would want to be sure to do

00:00:44.891 --> 00:00:47.235
is whether you like it or not,

00:00:47.235 --> 00:00:50.867
get very comfortable, it make take awhile,

00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:52.503
with public speaking for example.

00:00:52.503 --> 00:00:56.670
I mean, that's an asset that
will last you 50 or 60 years

00:00:57.837 --> 00:01:00.461
and it's a liability if
you don't like doing it

00:01:00.461 --> 00:01:02.624
or are uncomfortable doing
it that will also last you

00:01:02.624 --> 00:01:05.350
50 or 60 years and it's a necessary skill.

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:08.810
- Skill number two is
being honest with yourself.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:11.650
- One of the big things that
all startups do is they lie

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:14.968
to themselves, over and over and over.

00:01:14.968 --> 00:01:17.587
Mine's faster, mine's
cheaper, mine's better,

00:01:17.587 --> 00:01:19.523
mine's this, mine's that.

00:01:19.523 --> 00:01:22.334
No it's not and the
reason it's not is because

00:01:22.334 --> 00:01:24.686
whoever it is you're competing with,

00:01:24.686 --> 00:01:26.883
it's not like they're ignoring you.

00:01:26.883 --> 00:01:29.136
It's not like, oh my goodness,

00:01:29.136 --> 00:01:32.041
this guy just started on Shopify
in the startup competition

00:01:32.041 --> 00:01:33.915
and he's doing a million
dollars this year,

00:01:33.915 --> 00:01:36.513
woe is me, I might as
well close up the doors.

00:01:36.513 --> 00:01:37.346
What are they doing?

00:01:37.346 --> 00:01:39.135
I'm going to copy what they're
doing and now you've got to

00:01:39.135 --> 00:01:41.595
stay ahead and so, you
know, you've got to be

00:01:41.595 --> 00:01:43.918
very careful as a entrepreneur
to be brutally honest

00:01:43.918 --> 00:01:46.833
with yourself and those
are some of the things

00:01:46.833 --> 00:01:48.823
that you'll hear from me as a mentor.

00:01:48.823 --> 00:01:51.626
That know what you know,
know what you don't know,

00:01:51.626 --> 00:01:54.414
but you got to know your
business better than anybody.

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:57.687
- Skill number three is having confidence.

00:01:57.687 --> 00:01:59.502
- I don't know if you'd
agree with me on this,

00:01:59.502 --> 00:02:02.835
but in many ways, one of the key factors

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.150
to legendary success isn't
your natural ability.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.111
It's not whether you
have the right product,

00:02:11.111 --> 00:02:12.609
it's not whether you're
in the right field,

00:02:12.609 --> 00:02:15.372
it's not whether you've
had a blessed background.

00:02:15.372 --> 00:02:18.500
It's not whether you have the right IQ.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.661
I want you to really think
about and deconstruct

00:02:21.661 --> 00:02:24.695
and play with maybe later
tonight win your journal.

00:02:24.695 --> 00:02:28.519
I want you to deconstruct
this idea of confidence

00:02:28.519 --> 00:02:31.015
and it seems like a very simple
work but just think about

00:02:31.015 --> 00:02:32.571
it in your own life.

00:02:32.571 --> 00:02:35.959
When you have confidence or
we could even call it fire,

00:02:35.959 --> 00:02:39.158
when you have that fire
within you, that confidence,

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:40.991
that interior bravery,

00:02:43.837 --> 00:02:47.848
you almost have this power
to do whatever it takes

00:02:47.848 --> 00:02:50.348
to get your brave vision done.

00:02:51.488 --> 00:02:55.655
You see, in this world, it's
not about, in many ways,

00:02:56.728 --> 00:02:59.306
your strategy and your
business or your ability

00:02:59.306 --> 00:03:02.795
in your life, it's about
this thing called confidence

00:03:02.795 --> 00:03:05.440
and we've all had these times
in our lives when we are

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:08.594
full of confidence and what
other people see as a problem,

00:03:08.594 --> 00:03:11.126
we simply do see as an opportunity.

00:03:11.126 --> 00:03:14.612
Other people see it as a
stumbling block or a wall

00:03:14.612 --> 00:03:18.779
and we see it as a stepping
stone or this solution.

00:03:20.315 --> 00:03:23.102
So confidence is simply
something that you really

00:03:23.102 --> 00:03:24.107
want to wire in.

00:03:24.107 --> 00:03:26.542
Confidence is something
you really want to develop,

00:03:26.542 --> 00:03:29.888
confidence is a practice,
confidence is a muscle

00:03:29.888 --> 00:03:33.039
and like any muscle,
the more you focus on it

00:03:33.039 --> 00:03:37.179
and practice it and train it,
the stronger your confidence

00:03:37.179 --> 00:03:39.921
is going to grow and I
just have to say it again.

00:03:39.921 --> 00:03:42.169
When you are at a place in your life,

00:03:42.169 --> 00:03:46.336
when there is ongoing,
steady stream of confidence

00:03:47.177 --> 00:03:51.929
moving through your mindset,
moving though your heartset,

00:03:51.929 --> 00:03:53.771
you do the heroic in your business

00:03:53.771 --> 00:03:56.945
and you achieve the
remarkable in your life.

00:03:56.945 --> 00:03:59.012
- Skill number four is listening.

00:03:59.012 --> 00:04:03.179
- Nelson Mandela is a
particularly special case study

00:04:04.513 --> 00:04:08.355
in the leadership world because
he is universally regarded

00:04:08.355 --> 00:04:10.019
as a great leader.

00:04:10.019 --> 00:04:12.650
You can take other personalities
and depending on the nation

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:14.592
you go to, we have different
opinions about other

00:04:14.592 --> 00:04:17.634
personalities but Nelson
Mandela, across the world,

00:04:17.634 --> 00:04:21.049
is universally regarded as a great leader.

00:04:21.049 --> 00:04:24.808
He was actually the son of a tribal chief

00:04:24.808 --> 00:04:26.758
and he was asked one day,

00:04:26.758 --> 00:04:30.747
how did you learn to be a great leader?

00:04:30.747 --> 00:04:33.884
And he responded that he
would go with his father

00:04:33.884 --> 00:04:37.802
to tribal meetings and
he remembers two things

00:04:37.802 --> 00:04:40.295
when his father would
meet with other elders.

00:04:40.295 --> 00:04:43.462
One, they would always sit in a circle

00:04:44.745 --> 00:04:48.828
and two, his father was
always the last to speak.

00:04:50.697 --> 00:04:52.543
You will be told your
whole life that you need

00:04:52.543 --> 00:04:53.921
to learn to listen.

00:04:53.921 --> 00:04:58.026
I would say that you need to
learn to be the last to speak.

00:04:58.026 --> 00:05:00.312
I see it in board rooms
every day of the week.

00:05:00.312 --> 00:05:02.539
Even people who consider
themselves good leaders who may

00:05:02.539 --> 00:05:05.482
actually be decent leaders
will walk into a room and say,

00:05:05.482 --> 00:05:06.972
here's the problem, here's what I think,

00:05:06.972 --> 00:05:08.284
but I'm interested in your opinion,

00:05:08.284 --> 00:05:09.593
let's go around the room.

00:05:09.593 --> 00:05:11.299
It's too late.

00:05:11.299 --> 00:05:13.757
The skill to hold your
opinions to yourself until

00:05:13.757 --> 00:05:16.707
everyone has spoken does two things.

00:05:16.707 --> 00:05:19.126
One, it gives everybody else the feeling

00:05:19.126 --> 00:05:21.474
that they have been heard.

00:05:21.474 --> 00:05:23.463
It gives everyone else the ability to feel

00:05:23.463 --> 00:05:26.497
that they have contributed.

00:05:26.497 --> 00:05:29.182
And two, you get the benefit
of hearing what everybody

00:05:29.182 --> 00:05:32.187
else has to think before
you render your opinion.

00:05:32.187 --> 00:05:35.439
The skill is really to keep
your opinions to yourself.

00:05:35.439 --> 00:05:38.860
If you agree with somebody, don't nod yes.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:42.453
If you disagree with
somebody, don't nod no.

00:05:42.453 --> 00:05:44.258
Simply sit there, take it
all in, and the only thing

00:05:44.258 --> 00:05:47.547
you're allowed to do is ask
questions so that you can

00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:51.086
understand what they mean
and why they have the opinion

00:05:51.086 --> 00:05:54.873
that they have, you must
understand from where they

00:05:54.873 --> 00:05:58.301
are speaking, why they
have the opinion they have,

00:05:58.301 --> 00:06:00.812
not just what they are saying

00:06:00.812 --> 00:06:04.483
and at the end, you will get your turn.

00:06:04.483 --> 00:06:06.566
It sounds easy, it's not.

00:06:08.449 --> 00:06:10.763
Practice being the last to speak.

00:06:10.763 --> 00:06:13.164
That's what Nelson Mandela did.

00:06:13.164 --> 00:06:16.636
- Skill number five is managing your time.

00:06:16.636 --> 00:06:20.929
- My challenge in our
generation is that gradually

00:06:20.929 --> 00:06:24.262
through entertainment, through
television, through media,

00:06:24.262 --> 00:06:26.621
through every way possible,
we are living in a

00:06:26.621 --> 00:06:29.871
generation of the dumbing down of ideas

00:06:31.315 --> 00:06:35.482
because we have traded
effectiveness for busyness.

00:06:37.826 --> 00:06:41.993
Statistics say, yeah somebody
ought to clap on that.

00:06:43.977 --> 00:06:47.100
We are busier than any other
generation we have seen

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:49.600
in the last three to 400 years.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:54.189
We are so busy, we are
busier than a one armed

00:06:54.189 --> 00:06:57.611
wallpaper hanger, we're just
busy, you'll get it later,

00:06:57.611 --> 00:06:59.862
don't worry about it,
it'll hit you in a minute.

00:06:59.862 --> 00:07:02.669
We are just as busy as
we can be and we think

00:07:02.669 --> 00:07:05.438
because we're busy, we're effective.

00:07:05.438 --> 00:07:08.382
But I want you to challenge
your schedule for a minute

00:07:08.382 --> 00:07:12.921
and ask yourself, are you
really being effective

00:07:12.921 --> 00:07:16.815
or is your life cluttered
with all kinds of stuff

00:07:16.815 --> 00:07:20.708
that demands you and
drains you and taxes you

00:07:20.708 --> 00:07:23.788
and stops you from being
your highest and best self

00:07:23.788 --> 00:07:28.163
and are you substituting
busyness and all the chaos

00:07:28.163 --> 00:07:31.164
that goes along with busyness
from being effective?

00:07:31.164 --> 00:07:32.868
Let me tell you, a bunch
of scientists got together,

00:07:32.868 --> 00:07:35.719
they begin to do some research
and they begin to determine

00:07:35.719 --> 00:07:39.886
that 80% of the things we do
are busy things that we do

00:07:40.756 --> 00:07:43.733
in an area that is not effective,
that the average person

00:07:43.733 --> 00:07:48.001
only spends 20% of their time
doing the thing that they

00:07:48.001 --> 00:07:52.121
are really gifted, creative
at, passionate about,

00:07:52.121 --> 00:07:55.774
excited to do and the rest
of it is all the dismal,

00:07:55.774 --> 00:07:59.254
dumb stuff that we all have
to do in order to survive.

00:07:59.254 --> 00:08:00.997
Just crazy stuff that we're doing.

00:08:00.997 --> 00:08:04.283
Wonder what would happen
if we would go from doing

00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:06.890
80% of things that are
busy but not effective

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:09.295
and 20% of the things
that are really effective,

00:08:09.295 --> 00:08:12.479
if we would switch those
numbers around and only give

00:08:12.479 --> 00:08:14.982
20% of our time to the
things that we have to do

00:08:14.982 --> 00:08:18.233
and 80% of our time to thing that we were

00:08:18.233 --> 00:08:19.400
created to do.

00:08:20.552 --> 00:08:23.552
(audience applause)

00:08:25.324 --> 00:08:26.721
Wonder what would happen to your life.

00:08:26.721 --> 00:08:29.138
Now think about it a minute,
there's a lot of things

00:08:29.138 --> 00:08:30.568
you could take from me
and I could make it.

00:08:30.568 --> 00:08:32.374
You could take my suit, I got another one.

00:08:32.374 --> 00:08:34.603
You could take my car,
I could get another one.

00:08:34.603 --> 00:08:37.392
You could take my house,
I could get another house.

00:08:37.392 --> 00:08:41.559
But when you take my time, you
have taken something from me

00:08:42.865 --> 00:08:45.365
that is totally irreplaceable.

00:08:46.365 --> 00:08:49.841
We take all kinds of questions
from money management.

00:08:49.841 --> 00:08:52.306
We know how to manage our
money, we know how to repair

00:08:52.306 --> 00:08:55.651
our houses, we're working
on our hair and our bodies

00:08:55.651 --> 00:08:56.890
and all of this kind of stuff.

00:08:56.890 --> 00:08:59.478
We do everything except
the most important thing

00:08:59.478 --> 00:09:01.228
is to value our time.

00:09:02.955 --> 00:09:05.372
It takes time to be creative.

00:09:07.025 --> 00:09:09.456
You were meant to be creative.

00:09:09.456 --> 00:09:11.306
You were created in the
likeness and the image

00:09:11.306 --> 00:09:15.168
of a creator and in that
likeness and in that image,

00:09:15.168 --> 00:09:16.835
you have creativity.

00:09:17.947 --> 00:09:21.402
If you had time, you would be creative

00:09:21.402 --> 00:09:24.040
but in the absence of
time and with busyness

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:25.710
and clutter the ring ring
ring, the phone ring,

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:26.612
beep, beep, beep.

00:09:26.612 --> 00:09:28.832
You all got music playing on
your phone, all kinds of stuff.

00:09:28.832 --> 00:09:30.137
No matter kind of song you put on,

00:09:30.137 --> 00:09:32.386
after awhile you hate to
hear it because every time

00:09:32.386 --> 00:09:34.678
you hear that sound you
know it's somebody else

00:09:34.678 --> 00:09:38.318
wanting something else from
you that's taking you away

00:09:38.318 --> 00:09:41.775
from what you are gifted
and created to do.

00:09:41.775 --> 00:09:45.164
- Skill number six is stop whining.

00:09:45.164 --> 00:09:47.324
- So I have this thing that
I've been promoting called

00:09:47.324 --> 00:09:49.157
No Whining Wednesdays.

00:09:51.059 --> 00:09:55.226
Wednesday you cannot whine,
complain, or criticize.

00:09:57.506 --> 00:10:00.299
And people are like, well what is whining?

00:10:00.299 --> 00:10:04.083
Whatever you think it is, don't do it.

00:10:04.083 --> 00:10:05.958
And every time you do it,

00:10:05.958 --> 00:10:09.123
you have to put a quarter in a jar.

00:10:09.123 --> 00:10:12.401
If you whine about anything,
I can't, I'm tired,

00:10:12.401 --> 00:10:14.234
why me, why don't you?

00:10:17.631 --> 00:10:19.859
Every one of them will cost you a quarter.

00:10:19.859 --> 00:10:22.621
Or complaining, why don't
you, why do you always,

00:10:22.621 --> 00:10:25.034
how come you, I am always, they never.

00:10:25.034 --> 00:10:27.223
Oop, cost you a quarter.

00:10:27.223 --> 00:10:30.467
Then last week, I had people
send me pictures of their

00:10:30.467 --> 00:10:32.612
jars of quarters.

00:10:32.612 --> 00:10:35.710
Oh my god. (laughs)

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:39.397
What they said to me also
was, this was wonderful

00:10:39.397 --> 00:10:43.370
because I was never, ever
aware of how much whining

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:46.702
and complaining and criticizing I do.

00:10:46.702 --> 00:10:50.649
I've got mothers doing
it with their children.

00:10:50.649 --> 00:10:54.154
So Wednesday is No Whining Wednesday.

00:10:54.154 --> 00:10:56.076
We need to practice that.

00:10:56.076 --> 00:11:00.243
We need to stop whining
about what isn't happening,

00:11:01.324 --> 00:11:02.889
what we don't have, what we can't do,

00:11:02.889 --> 00:11:04.889
what somebody didn't do.

00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:08.270
We got to get it clean in 2014.

00:11:09.191 --> 00:11:12.774
No whining, no complaining,
no criticizing.

00:11:15.584 --> 00:11:19.751
- Skill number seven is
staying present in the moment.

00:15:28.137 --> 00:15:31.626
Skill number eight, being consistent.

00:15:31.626 --> 00:15:34.438
- Success is not a destination.

00:15:34.438 --> 00:15:36.938
People always think that
oh my god, this person's

00:15:36.938 --> 00:15:38.849
so successful because they've
had a successful movie

00:15:38.849 --> 00:15:41.851
or a successful show but
success is a journey.

00:15:41.851 --> 00:15:44.684
You have to consistently
always, always be successful.

00:15:44.684 --> 00:15:46.431
That's when you're really
successful because otherwise

00:15:46.431 --> 00:15:48.816
you'll be remembered for your last failure

00:15:48.816 --> 00:15:52.536
and I've had a few but I
just have to compensate for

00:15:52.536 --> 00:15:55.984
my failure by just getting up and running.

00:15:55.984 --> 00:15:58.799
The more time you take
to sort of mourn it,

00:15:58.799 --> 00:16:02.554
you're wasting that much more
time in being successful.

00:16:02.554 --> 00:16:06.019
So you just have to perk
up, pick yourself up,

00:16:06.019 --> 00:16:08.351
dust yourself off and
just say what can I do?

00:16:08.351 --> 00:16:09.620
There's always a solution.

00:16:09.620 --> 00:16:12.669
You can't expect, people
always get mistaken that

00:16:12.669 --> 00:16:15.829
just because this year
has been what it has been,

00:16:15.829 --> 00:16:18.410
it's been because I
consistently worked hard

00:16:18.410 --> 00:16:20.015
for about 10 years.

00:16:20.015 --> 00:16:22.281
So now, I know my job enough to be able

00:16:22.281 --> 00:16:24.092
to be appreciated for it.

00:16:24.092 --> 00:16:26.198
But if I hadn't had the last 10 years,

00:16:26.198 --> 00:16:29.646
I would never have had this
year or the year before that.

00:16:29.646 --> 00:16:32.090
So success is not a destination where you,

00:16:32.090 --> 00:16:35.767
or power is not like okay
today you're powerful.

00:16:35.767 --> 00:16:37.861
You have to consistently be powerful

00:16:37.861 --> 00:16:41.735
and consistently be successful
and that is a journey.

00:16:41.735 --> 00:16:44.735
- Skill number nine is
getting enough sleep.

00:16:44.735 --> 00:16:47.986
- Well, first of all, I prioritize sleep.

00:16:47.986 --> 00:16:52.155
That means saying no to
things you want to do.

00:16:52.155 --> 00:16:53.165
It's not easy.

00:16:53.165 --> 00:16:55.054
- [Interviewer] No, it's hard,
I think the hardest thing.

00:16:55.054 --> 00:16:58.219
- Last night, you know,
Sheryl Sandberg interviewed me

00:16:58.219 --> 00:17:02.436
at the San Fran Symphony Hall
and then I had to sign books

00:17:02.436 --> 00:17:05.199
and I would have loved to
go and have dinner with her

00:17:05.199 --> 00:17:06.699
but I went to bed.

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:12.969
You know, because if I
had gone out to dinner

00:17:12.969 --> 00:17:17.508
and hadn't gotten my at least
seven hours sleep that I need

00:17:17.508 --> 00:17:19.478
and had to get up early to do TV,

00:17:19.478 --> 00:17:21.994
I would not be enjoying
being here with you now

00:17:21.994 --> 00:17:24.482
and I'm really enjoying
it and I'm 100% present

00:17:24.482 --> 00:17:27.036
and I'm not tired and I
hate being tired more than

00:17:27.036 --> 00:17:28.555
I hate anything.

00:17:28.555 --> 00:17:31.412
- And skill number 10 is having empathy.

00:17:31.412 --> 00:17:35.412
- I've thought a lot about
the power of empathy.

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:40.577
In my work, it's the
current that connects me

00:17:40.577 --> 00:17:44.580
and my actual pulse to
a fictional character

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:48.747
in a made up story, it allows
me to feel pretend feelings

00:17:49.850 --> 00:17:52.350
and sorrows and imagined pain.

00:17:53.695 --> 00:17:57.445
In my nervous system is
sympathetically wired

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:02.467
and it conducts that current to you

00:18:02.467 --> 00:18:04.506
sitting in a movie theater

00:18:04.506 --> 00:18:07.632
and to the woman sitting next to you

00:18:07.632 --> 00:18:12.325
and to her friend so that we
all feel that it's happening

00:18:12.325 --> 00:18:14.126
to us at the same time.

00:18:14.126 --> 00:18:16.481
It's a very mysterious
and valuable resource

00:18:16.481 --> 00:18:18.619
of the human species

00:18:18.619 --> 00:18:22.619
and women, I think, access
it most effortlessly.

00:18:24.772 --> 00:18:28.714
We cry at sad movies, we
don't feel we lose face

00:18:28.714 --> 00:18:31.381
or stature or position doing it.

00:18:33.425 --> 00:18:38.064
We see a news story that
enrages us and we write letters

00:18:38.064 --> 00:18:40.394
through tears, our hearts pounding.

00:18:40.394 --> 00:18:44.561
I often used to wonder why
human beings developed these

00:18:45.657 --> 00:18:48.990
inconvenient and embarrassing responses,

00:18:50.027 --> 00:18:53.444
this sniffling, choking, wet obstruction.

00:18:56.542 --> 00:18:59.879
You know, the thing
physicians and soldiers and

00:18:59.879 --> 00:19:03.931
stock traders and journalists
and fashion models

00:19:03.931 --> 00:19:06.733
and politicians and news commentators

00:19:06.733 --> 00:19:09.266
and venture capitalists
all must suppress in order

00:19:09.266 --> 00:19:10.958
to work most efficiently.

00:19:10.958 --> 00:19:14.098
(audience laughing)

00:19:14.098 --> 00:19:19.007
I thought what possible
value, function could it serve

00:19:19.007 --> 00:19:21.402
in the Darwinian scheme of you know,

00:19:21.402 --> 00:19:23.515
survival of the fittest and the strongest

00:19:23.515 --> 00:19:26.043
and the most heavily armed.

00:19:26.043 --> 00:19:27.767
(audience laughing)

00:19:27.767 --> 00:19:32.621
No seriously, I thought
why and how did we evolve

00:19:32.621 --> 00:19:36.121
with this weak and useless passion in tact

00:19:38.921 --> 00:19:41.338
within the deep heart's core.

00:19:43.556 --> 00:19:46.553
And the answer as I've
formulated it to myself

00:19:46.553 --> 00:19:48.970
is that empathy is the engine

00:19:50.515 --> 00:19:53.098
that powers all the best in us.

00:19:54.422 --> 00:19:56.422
It is what civilizes us.

00:19:57.690 --> 00:19:59.902
It is what connects us.

00:19:59.902 --> 00:20:00.919
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:20:00.919 --> 00:20:02.015
I hope you enjoyed.

00:20:02.015 --> 00:20:04.913
I'd love to know what did
you think of this video?

00:20:04.913 --> 00:20:06.976
What did you take from it
that you're going to apply

00:20:06.976 --> 00:20:10.000
immediately somehow to your
life or to your business?

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:12.042
What was your favorite from the 10

00:20:12.042 --> 00:20:13.591
and how you're going to apply?

00:20:13.591 --> 00:20:15.292
Leave it down on the comments below,

00:20:15.292 --> 00:20:16.622
I'm really curious to find out.

00:20:16.622 --> 00:20:19.490
Also if you think there's
11, 12, 13 that you want to

00:20:19.490 --> 00:20:21.572
add to the list, leave
it down on the comments.

00:20:21.572 --> 00:20:23.648
I'm super curious to see
what you have to say.

00:20:23.648 --> 00:20:25.944
Thank you guys again for
watching, I believe in you.

00:20:25.944 --> 00:20:28.063
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:20:28.063 --> 00:20:29.978
and whatever your one word is.

00:20:29.978 --> 00:20:32.395
Much love, I'll see you soon.

